// koru/vercel — host a site on Vercel through Orisha.
//
// Pure Koru, front and center. This file is the contract: nothing here is Zig,
// and nothing here touches a socket, a file descriptor, or a platform. The Zig
// half of this library — the wasm reactor host seam (the exported request/response
// windows and the `handle` function) — is generated by `koru-vercel build` into
// your build directory as a thin entry. It is plumbing, so it is fine as .kz;
// you never write it, and it is never this file.
//
// Why this split is correct: Vercel gives a deployment no socket. It accepts the
// connection, parses HTTP, and calls your function once with a request. Orisha
// already split the same way (orisha/lib/index.k) — `answer` turns request bytes
// into response bytes and never names a descriptor; the pump owns the loop. On a
// serverless host the platform owns the loop and you keep only the answer half,
// compiled to a wasm reactor.
//
// A completely static site needs no Koru of your own at all:
//
//     koru-vercel build ./public
//     koru-vercel deploy
//
// That embeds `public` into the wasm module at compile time and serves it as
// pre-rendered HTTP — no per-request parsing, no runtime dispatch, no libc.
//
// If you want Koru in the loop, author a `main.k` beside your static root. It is
// pure Koru and it becomes the whole server:

//   ~import orisha
//   ~orisha:static(name: "site", root: "public", fallback: "200.html")
//   ~orisha:handler = orisha:static-router(name: "site")

import std/build

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The handler contract the generated reactor calls.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A site is a function from a request path to a response body. An empty body is
// a 404. Implement `body-for` here (pure Koru) for dynamic-but-small routes; a
// purely static site leaves it to Orisha's embedded static router.
[abstract|retain] pub tor body-for { path: string } -> string

body-for -> ""
